Now, this diagnosis has a consequence that goes well beyond the red-card study, and Auspurg and Brüderl put it in their title: has the credibility of the social sciences been credibly destroyed? If the twenty-nine teams were in fact answering four different questions, then most of their disagreement is not evidence that data analysis is unreliable. It is evidence that the question they were handed was under-specified, and that is an ordinary, fixable defect of research design. Read that way, the whole many-analyst literature stops being a verdict on the field and becomes a diagnosis of vague research questions. I find that a considerably more optimistic reading. It is also a more demanding one, because it takes the problem away from statistics, where you could hope a better estimator would save you, and puts it back on whoever writes the question, which in the tasks ahead means you.
