## The specification menu {#the-specification-menu .smaller}

::: {.fs-55}
| Level | Decision | On the curve | Off the curve |
|:--|:--|:--|:--|
| 1 – Estimands | **Outcome** | Greeted by name | Any reply · Reply within 24 hours *(another question)* |
|  | **Comparison group** | White names · White and Black names | Black names *(another question)* |
|  | **Emails counted** | Replies only · All delivered emails | – |
|  | **Names** | Male and female names | Male names only · Female names only *(another question)* |
|  | **Who replied** | Any official | Officials with Hispanic names *(another question)* |
| 2 – Measurement | **Replies without a greeting code** | Leave out · Count as no greeting | – |
|  | **Bounced emails** | Leave out | Count as no reply *(a weaker answer)* |
| 3 – Estimation | **Model** | Difference in proportions · Logistic regression | Three-group model *(a check)* |
|  | **Adjustment** | No covariates · Hard-to-find address · Place, without ethnic composition · Place, with ethnic composition | Sex of the name *(a check)*; Continuous census values *(not in the public file)* |
|  | **Test** | The test of the model | Randomisation test · Chi-square test *(a check)*; One-tailed test *(a weaker answer)* |
|  | **Effect scale** | Percentage points | Odds ratio · Partial correlation *(a check)* |
:::

::: {.small}
Five decisions keep more than one option, and they cross into the **64** specifications of the curve. The [decision map](../cases/housing/map.html) gives the reason for every option.
:::

::: notes
Here is the menu, and everything that follows is built on it. It is the full list of decisions that any analyst of this claim has to take, eleven of them, at three levels. Level 1 decides which question is asked: the outcome, the comparison group, which emails are counted, which names, and whose replies. Level 2 decides how the outcome is measured: what to do with the five replies whose greeting nobody coded, and with the emails that bounced. Level 3 decides how the answer is reached: the model, the covariates, the test and the scale of the result. Every option on the right was considered and left off the curve for a stated reason: it answers another question, it answers this one less well, it is a check, or it is simply not in the public file. The decision map on the site gives the reason for each.
:::

## The curve of 64 {#the-curve-of-64 .smaller}

::: {.small}
- **Comparison group** – white names · white and Black names
- **Emails counted** – replies only · all delivered emails
- **Replies without a greeting code** – left out · counted as no greeting
- **Model** – a difference in proportions · logistic regression
- **Adjustment** – none · hard-to-find address · place, without ethnic composition · place, with ethnic composition
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 4 = **64**. The reference specification is the raw gap of the paper: 41.2% against 61.1%, **−19.97 points**, *t* = −3.87 on 360 df, n = 362 replies.

```{.r}
try_spec(comparison = "white", population = "replies", uncoded = "drop",
         model = "lpm", adjust = "none")
```
:::

::: notes
Five of the eleven decisions keep more than one option on the curve. The comparison, with white names or with white and Black names. The emails counted, replies or every delivered email. The five uncoded replies, left out or counted as replies without a greeting. The model, a difference in proportions or a logistic regression. And the adjustment, four sets of covariates from none to the set of the original model, in the thirds that the public file holds. [advance] Two times two times two times two times four is sixty-four specifications, and the course has fitted every one. The reference specification is the raw gap of the paper, Hispanic against white names among the replies, with no covariates: forty-one against sixty-one per cent, a gap of twenty points. In the helpers, every argument of try underscore spec defaults to that reference, so changing one thing means changing one argument.
:::

## Ground rules for the menu {#one-deviation-and-a-reason-for-it .smaller}

::: {.small}
- **Change one decision at a time**, and hold the others at the reference specification. When a result moves, you can then say what moved it
- **An option off the curve is refused** by `try_spec()`, with the reason from the menu and the page that teaches it. Asking for one is part of the exercise
- **One sign convention:** every estimate is Hispanic minus comparison names, in percentage points, so below zero is the direction of the claim
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
The estimand statement on the [decision map](../cases/housing/map.html) rewrites itself as you choose. A change in its first sentence asks another question, a change inside its parentheses counts the outcome differently, and a change in the second sentence reaches the answer another way.
:::

::: notes
Three ground rules before you touch the menu. First, change one decision at a time and hold the others at the reference. That sounds restrictive, but it is what makes the class chart readable, because when a result sits somewhere surprising we can say what put it there. Second, if you ask for an option that is off the curve, try underscore spec refuses it, tells you why, and names the page that teaches it. Trying one is part of the exercise, because the refusal is where the reasoning lives. Third, one sign convention binds everybody: every estimate is the Hispanic names minus the comparison names, in percentage points, so below zero is the direction of the claim. [advance] And the decision map on the site shows the estimand statement rewriting itself as you choose, which tells you whether a choice changes the question or only the way of answering it.
:::

## A difference in proportions {#a-difference-in-proportions .smaller}

::: {.small}
- Among the replies with a greeting code, **41.2%** of the 187 replies to Hispanic names opened with the name of the sender, against **61.1%** of the 175 replies to white names
- The difference: **−19.97 percentage points**
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
A linear model of a 0/1 outcome on a 0/1 indicator of the group gives exactly that difference as its coefficient. This is the **linear probability model**, and its coefficient reads directly in the units of the claim.

```{.r}
lm(named ~ hispanic, data = replies)    # the coefficient is the difference, as a proportion
```
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
Its test, with HC2 standard errors: *t* = −3.87 on 360 df, p = 0.0001.
:::

::: notes
The statistics of this case are about as simple as statistics gets, which is why it is a good case to learn on. Among the coded replies, forty-one per cent of those to Hispanic names opened with the name of the sender, against sixty-one per cent of those to white names. The difference is twenty percentage points, and that difference is the estimate. [advance] You can get the same number from a regression. Fit a linear model of the greeting, coded zero or one, on an indicator that is one for the Hispanic names, and its coefficient is exactly the difference between the two shares. That model is called the linear probability model, and its great virtue is that its coefficient is already in the units of the claim. [advance] Its test, with the standard errors that the course uses throughout, gives a t of minus three point eight seven.
:::

## Linear probability model and logit {#linear-probability-model-and-logit .smaller}

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="50%"}
::: {.small}
**Linear probability model**

- The coefficient is the difference in proportions
- Simple, and in the units of the claim
- With covariates it can predict shares below 0 or above 1
:::
:::
::: {.column width="50%"}
::: {.small}
**Logistic regression (logit)**

- Models the log odds of a greeting; the coefficient of the Hispanic names is −0.810, with *z* = −3.771 (UYV3F)
- Its **average marginal effect**, the change in the predicted share averaged over the emails, is on the course scale
- Predicted shares always stay between 0 and 1
:::
:::
::::

::: {.fragment .small}
Without covariates a logit reproduces the two observed shares exactly, so its average marginal effect is the same −19.97 points. Across the curve, the logit moves the estimate by −0.01 points on average against its linear twin.
:::

::: notes
The paper itself used a different model, the logistic regression or logit, which models the log odds of a greeting rather than its share. Its coefficient, minus point eight one here, is on the scale of log odds, and the z that goes with it is exactly the value that Multi100 recorded for UYV3F. To put a logit on the scale of the claim, you take its average marginal effect: predict the share of greetings for every email as if it carried a Hispanic name and as if it carried a white one, and average the difference. [advance] With no covariates the logit reproduces the two observed shares exactly, so its average marginal effect is the same twenty points. With covariates the two models part a little, and across the whole curve the choice between them moves the estimate by a hundredth of a point on average. The model is a decision on the menu that barely matters here.
:::

