## Robustness and replicability {#robustness-and-replicability}

:::: {.none .fs-85}
::: {.fragment}
On the original data, every justifiable analysis finds the gap. Only its size moves.
:::

::: {.fragment}
Every one of those analyses uses the same 1,014 emails, the same six names, the same officials in the same year. **The curve varies the analysis and never the sample.**
:::

::: {.fragment}
So robustness to analytic choices on one sample is not replicability.
:::

::: {.fragment}
Candidates for the difference: the **names** [@Gaddis2017HowBlackAre], **chance**, and **time and place**.
:::
::::

::: notes
This is the lesson of the case, and of the course. On the original data the claim is hard to dislodge: every justifiable analysis finds the gap, and only its size moves. [advance] But every one of those analyses works on the same thousand emails, the same six names, the same officials in the same week of 2014. A specification curve varies the analysis and holds the sample fixed, so it cannot tell you what a new sample would show. [advance] Robustness to analytic choices on one sample is a different property from replicability. [advance] Why might the two studies differ? The names are one candidate: names that are meant to indicate one group are not all read the same way, and Santiago and Gabriela may also have read as younger. Chance is another, given the power of the original design. And time and place are a third. The replication does not tell us which.
:::

## Critics of the multiverse {#the-multiverse-has-its-critics .smaller}

::: {.small}
- "Robustness is better assessed with a **few thoughtful models** than with billions of regressions" [@Auspurg2025RobustnessBetterAssessed; answering @GanslmeierVlandas2025EstimatingExtentSources] – only *justified* models targeting the *same estimand* belong in one curve. The curve of this course keeps only the specifications that a review of every option judged justifiable for the claim, and setting each option beside its twins does the job that every critic endorses: it locates where the argument must happen
- "**There is only one correct analysis**" once the question is fully specified [@LakensEtAl2026ThereOnlyOne] – your preregistered specification guards against *cherry-picking*, and their deeper point is that preregistering a choice does not yet make it *justified*, which is what the estimand and DAG work were for
- "What's a multiverse **good for** anyway?" – reflection, persuasion, or inference, and each purpose needs a *differently built* universe [@RohrerEtAl2026WhatsMultiverseGood]. The full menu serves **reflection** and the curve of the claim moves towards **persuasion**; how much more to read into it is the open debate
:::

::: {.src}
The book-length how-to: @YoungCumberworth2025MultiverseAnalysisComputational; the estimand diagnosis of the red-card study: @AuspurgBruderl2021HasCredibilitySocial.
:::

::: notes
The method you practised is the subject of a live debate, and you can now read that debate for yourself. Katrin Auspurg, who with Josef Brüderl traced the spread in the red-card study to teams answering different questions, answered a 3.6-billion-estimate study with the line at the top of this slide: only thoughtful, justified models belong in a curve. The curve of this course is built the way she recommends. Every option of the menu was examined for whether it is a justifiable answer to the claim, the curve keeps only the options that passed, and setting each option beside its twins does the one job that every critic endorses, which is to show where the argument has to happen. Lakens and colleagues say that once the question is fixed, only one analysis implements it correctly. A preregistered specification guards against cherry-picking. Their deeper point, that preregistering a choice does not make it justified, is why the DAG comes before the preregistration. And Rohrer, Hullman and Gelman ask what a multiverse is for: reflection, persuasion, or inference, each needing a differently built set. The full menu serves reflection, and the curve of the claim, with a published reason for every option, moves towards persuasion; how much more can be read into it is the open question in the debate you have now joined. [advance]
:::

## The one-correct-analysis argument {#there-is-only-one-correct-analysis .smaller}

::: {.small}
The argument from Lakens and colleagues runs in four steps:

- A research question, **fully specified**, implies a quantity to be estimated
- That quantity, together with the design and the assumptions you are willing to make, implies **one analysis** that estimates it
- So a spread of results is not evidence of legitimate diversity. It is evidence that **most of those specifications are wrong** – they estimate something else
- On this reading, showing a wide curve is closer to an admission than to a robustness check
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
Take it back to the red cards. Twenty-nine teams modelled one count of red cards as **linear, logistic or Poisson**. Those are claims about how the outcome was generated, and they cannot all be right – so on this argument most of the spread is error.
:::

::: {.src}
@LakensEtAl2026ThereOnlyOne
:::

::: notes
The strongest objection to the multiverse approach comes from Lakens and colleagues, and I want to give it its full force. They argue that a research question, once fully specified, implies a quantity to be estimated. That quantity, together with your design and the assumptions you are prepared to make, implies one analysis that estimates it. From which it follows that a spread of results is evidence that most of those specifications are wrong, because they estimate something other than what you said you wanted, and not evidence of legitimate analytical diversity at all. And on that reading, publishing a wide specification curve is closer to an admission that you never specified your question than to a robustness check. That is an uncomfortable argument and I think it is largely right.
:::

## Identifying the one correct analysis {#how-would-you-know .smaller}

:::: {.none}
::: {.fragment}
Grant the argument, and suppose that **one correct analysis exists**. Which one is it?
:::

::: {.fragment}
Getting there needs the estimand *and* the identification assumptions *and* agreement about the measurement – and reasonable people can disagree about each of the three.
:::

::: {.fragment}
So the curve complements the one-correct-analysis view: it is a **map of the disagreement that remains**, drawn because we cannot yet settle it.
:::

::: {.fragment}
And it points at what to argue about: the **few decisions that move the estimate**.
:::
::::

::: notes
So let us grant the argument. Suppose one correct analysis does exist. The next question is which one it is, and how you would know. To get there you need the estimand, and the identification assumptions, and agreement about how the construct is measured – and reasonable people can disagree about each of the three, as you found for yourselves in the hands-on tasks. So I read the curve as a companion to the one-correct-analysis view rather than a rival to it. It is a map of the disagreement that remains, drawn because we cannot yet settle it. Read that way it is useful, because it shows where the disagreement is, in the few decisions that move the estimate, and on our claim we have already found the one that matters most. Those are arguments about the claim itself, and they belong in the paper rather than in a footnote about robustness.
:::

## What preregistration settles {.smaller}

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="50%"}
::: {.small}
**It does settle**

- That your specification was not chosen *because of* the result
- That your rule for what counts as support was fixed before you had a result
- That a reader can see what you intended, and what you changed
:::
:::
::: {.column width="50%"}
::: {.small}
**It does not settle**

- Whether the specification was **any good**
- Whether it estimates the quantity that your question implies
- Whether the assumptions behind it hold

A badly reasoned analysis that is preregistered is still badly reasoned; the preregistration only adds a timestamp
:::
:::
::::

::: {.fragment .small}
That is why the estimand and the DAG came **first** today: preregistration protects a choice, and the causal reasoning is what justifies it.
:::

::: notes
So where does preregistration fit? It settles a specific and limited set of things, and the two columns matter equally. On the left: it settles that your specification was not chosen because of the result it gave, that your rule for what counts as support was fixed before you had a result to bend it around, and that a reader can see both what you intended and what you subsequently changed. Those are real guarantees and no other mechanism provides them. On the right, what it does not settle: whether the specification was any good, whether it estimates the quantity that your question implies, or whether its assumptions hold. A badly reasoned analysis that is preregistered is still badly reasoned, only now with a timestamp on it. That is why we did the estimand and the graph first. Preregistration protects a choice, and the causal reasoning is what justifies it.
:::

